A||||5||XenoGen||What Size of Tires?||||5||
Z||000000||XenoGen||08-08-2000||11:05 AM||||<BR> Hi, I recently bought an 88 Fiero. So you know a little about it, it's a Canadian car, with a bit over 100,000 KMS, 4cyl engine, nice shape except for the paint on the front, ect. <P> The tires on the car are now pretty worn and it's time to get new tires. They are the stock sizes, but I want to go for soemthing bigger... I know there must be a better combination of slightly larger tire sizes out there that fit on the car no problem, and that increase the handling, and I was wodnering what that is.<P>I know putting a larger size on the back increases handling, ect.  <P>Could someone please tell me what (larger than stock) tire sizes I should get for better handling on my 88 Fiero? <P>Thanks!<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||overlord||08-08-2000||01:30 PM||||hey xeno,<BR>welcome to the club.<P>You didnt happen to pick the 88 up out of Winnipeg, Manitoba by any chance?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||08-08-2000||01:41 PM||||205-60-14 up front and 215-60-14 in the back.. thats what my SE came with (from the previous owner) i tossed on a set of Yokohama A509s 205-60-14 and LOVED it! check <A HREF="http://www.tirerack.com" TARGET=_blank>www.tirerack.com</A>  for the Yokohama AVS Intermediate in a 205-60-14... good ride and decent sidewall strength for great handling..  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||XenoGen||08-08-2000||02:28 PM||||<BR> I registered here on the Forum quite a while ago, and it was only recently that I got my Fiero. I liked Fieros before, but I was a little hesitant with getting one, but then I came accross the Forum and found out the answer to all my questions and concerns, and then I was convincved about getting one.<P>After a few months of searching, I found a nice one for a nice enough price... A little more than I wanted, but it had under 100,000 kms when I got it. (I paid 2300$ Canadian.)<BR>I got the car in the Maritimes. Where I live.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Okay, about the 205-60-14 front and 215-60-14 size tires, does that keep the same speedometer reading the same? It doesn't mess anything up? (I'll admit I don't know much about tires, I just know it can be an issue.)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||XenoGen||08-08-2000||02:41 PM||||<BR>Oh, what are good sizes to go for that are slightly bigger if I have 13 inch tires?<P>I don't think I do, but I actually never checked... I just assumed I had 14 inch.. But when I went to the yokohama site it has 13 inch wheels listed as the stock size...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||08-08-2000||07:44 PM||||more times than not you'll see that these sites have incorrect information on them... i don't think we ever were adorned with 13" wheels. i sure hope not anyway.. the GTs had 15s the SE and the coupes had 14s. your speed o will be fine with the different rubber. you'd really have to change sizes a lot to toss it off 5mph at 60... even if you had 185-75-14s on your car and you put 215-60s on it your speedo would be off... only 4tenths at 60. look on the same site unter tires and then under their manufactures name and the size.  <A HREF="http://www.discounttiredirect.com" TARGET=_blank>www.discounttiredirect.com</A>  will help too ||||reg||1
